      "id": 1,
      "text": "Overton Park was founded in 1901 as Memphis’s first metropolitan park, part of a nationwide green space movement sparked by NYC Central Park.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6,
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://overtonpark.org/the-park/park-history/"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The park was named for John Overton, a co‑founder of Memphis, after a newspaper contest in 1906.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        7,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2",
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.historic-memphis.com/memphis-historic/overtonpark/overtonpark.html"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "George Kessler designed Overton Park as part of a comprehensive plan that also included Riverside Park.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Overton_Park"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In 1971, Citizens to Preserve Overton Park won a landmark Supreme Court case that halted highway construction through the park.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
